.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAG-CREATE-ROUTE 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eag-create-route \- publish a path through the API gateway
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli eag create-route
.BR \-\-route\-id | \-r
.I ID
.BR \-\-path | \-p
.I PREFIX
.RB ( \-\-application | \-a
.I NAME
.RB | \-\-module | \-M
.I NAME
.B \-\-action
.IR NAME )
.RB [ \-\-methods | \-m
.IR LIST ]
.RB [ \-\-region | \-R
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-namespace | \-N
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-authentication | \-A
.IR none | euclid | basic ]
.RB [ \-\-inactive | \-i ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Publishes a path prefix on the API gateway's port and sends everything
beneath it to an application, one running instance at a time in turn.
.PP
The path is forwarded unchanged, so a route on
.I /resource
reaches the application as
.I /resource/searchById?id=123
\- the application sees the URL its callers typed, and nothing about the
route appears in it.
.PP
The instances are found from the ports the manager handed them, so an
application that scales from one instance to eight is spread across
eight backends within a few seconds without the route being touched.
.PP
The application must already exist. A route pointing at nothing would
answer 503 for every request, which looks like an application that is
down rather than one that was never deployed \- so this is refused with
404 instead.
.SH MATCHING
Longest prefix wins: a route on
.I /resource
carries everything below it, and a later, more specific
.I /resource/export
takes precedence for its own subtree.
.PP
A prefix only matches whole segments, so
.I /resource
never claims
.IR /resource\-intern .
.PP
Two routes may have the same path only if their methods do not overlap,
which is how reads and writes of one resource go to different
applications. An overlap is refused with 409: the winner would be
decided by whichever the gateway happened to consider first, and callers
would reach one application today and possibly the other tomorrow. A
route naming no methods answers for all of them and so overlaps with
everything.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-r ", " \-\-route\-id " " \fIID\fR
Name for the route, unique within the installation. Required. This is
how the route is referred to afterwards; it never appears in a URL.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-path " " \fIPREFIX\fR
Path prefix to publish, e.g. \fI/resource\fR. Required. Must
start with \fI/\fR \- a prefix that does not could never match a request
target, and would become a route that is configured, listed and silently
dead.
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-application " " \fINAME\fR
Application the requests are sent to, as deployed through
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1).
Either this or \fB\-\-module\fR, never both.
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-module " " \fINAME\fR
Euclid module the requests are sent to instead of an application \-
\fIeam\fR, \fIesm\fR, \fIeqs\fR and so on. Requires \fB\-\-action\fR. See
.B REACHING EUCLID ITSELF
in
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1).
.TP
.B \-\-action " " \fINAME\fR
The one action \fB\-\-module\fR answers for on this route, e.g.
\fIlogin\fR. One per route: a route cannot take its action from the
path, because that would publish every action the module has.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-methods " " \fILIST\fR
Comma-separated HTTP methods this route answers for, e.g.
\fIGET,POST\fR. Case does not matter; they are stored upper case. One
of \fIGET\fR, \fIHEAD\fR, \fIPOST\fR, \fIPUT\fR, \fIPATCH\fR,
\fIDELETE\fR or \fIOPTIONS\fR \- anything else is refused rather than
stored, since a typo here is a route that answers for nothing.
.RS
.PP
Omit it and the route answers for every method, including ones not in
that list. That is the point of leaving it empty rather than spelling
out today's methods: a route written now should not refuse whatever
comes next.
.PP
A request whose method no route on the path accepts is answered with
405 and an \fIAllow\fR header, not 404.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-R ", " \-\-region " " \fINAME\fR
Region requests on this route act in. Defaults to your own. The gateway
puts it on every request it forwards, so callers need not know euclid
has regions.
.TP
.BR \-N ", " \-\-namespace " " \fINAME\fR
Namespace requests on this route act in. Defaults to the one you are
working in. Worth naming when it differs: a route published for one
namespace should not act in another merely because an administrator of
the first configured it.
.TP
.BR \-A ", " \-\-authentication " " \fInone\fR|\fIeuclid\fR|\fIbasic\fR
What the gateway requires of a caller. Defaults to \fInone\fR.
.RS
.PP
With \fInone\fR every request is forwarded and whatever the application
requires it enforces itself \- which is what a route behind an external
API gateway wants, and what a public read wants.
.PP
With \fIeuclid\fR a euclid credential is required and verified before
anything is forwarded, and the check happens before a backend is chosen,
so an unauthenticated caller never causes a connection to an application
at all. That means a bearer token, an RFC 9421 signature or SigV4.
.PP
With \fIbasic\fR it is HTTP Basic instead, checked against the same
euclid user passwords, and an unauthenticated caller is answered with a
\fIWWW-Authenticate\fR header so a browser prompts for a username and
password. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1)
for what that costs and why only the gateway accepts it.
.RE
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-inactive
Create the route but leave it out of service. Nothing is served for its
path until
.B "update-route \-\-active true"
puts it in. Useful for preparing a cutover.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The route was created. Its definition is printed as JSON.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, a path that does not start with \fI/\fR,
an unrecognised
.B \-\-authentication
or
.BR \-\-methods ,
no administrator session, a route ID already in use, a path and method
already routed, an application that does not exist, or the server
rejected the request.
.SH EXAMPLES
Publish a parser's REST interface:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id resource \e
    --path /resource \e
    --application onix-parser
.fi
.RE
.PP
Carve out an administrative subtree of the same application, requiring a
euclid credential for it alone:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id resource-admin \e
    --path /resource/admin \e
    --application onix-parser \e
    --authentication euclid
.fi
.RE
.PP
Split one resource by method, reads to the parser and writes to another
application:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id produkte-lesen \e
    --path /api/produkte \e
    --application parser \e
    --methods GET,HEAD

euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id produkte-pflege \e
    --path /api/produkte \e
    --application pim-writer \e
    --methods POST,PUT,DELETE
.fi
.RE
.PP
Prepare a route without serving it yet:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id reports \e
    --path /reports \e
    --application report-service \e
    --inactive
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-eag (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-update-route (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-list-routes (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eag-delete-route (1),
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
